<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hudson Valley</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hudson Valley is the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, running north from New York City towards Albany. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settled it as the core of New Netherland, granting large patroonships such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rensselaerswyck">Rensselaerswyck</a>. Under English rule great manor estates dominated it, and Dutch language, place names and customs persisted there long after 1664.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hudson-valley.jpg" alt="Hudson Valley" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UpstateNYer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BrunswickAutumn.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The valley runs from the Capital District around Albany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> south to Yonkers in Westchester County, cutting through Triassic sandstones in the south and far older Precambrian gneiss in the north. In the Hudson Highlands the river enters a fjord cut in earlier ice ages, and near the Tappan Zee its west bank rises in diabase cliffs 400 to 800 feet high. Before European settlers arrived, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> lived along the lower river, the Wappingers from Manhattan Island up to Poughkeepsie, and the Mahicans in the north, with their capital near present-day Albany.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1609 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> financed the English navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-hudson">Henry Hudson</a>'s search for the Northwest Passage, and he sailed up the river as far as present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> before concluding that no strait lay there. Voyages by Adriaen Block between 1611 and 1614 showed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> would pay, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> founded three trading posts along the river: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>, Wiltwyck and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-orange">Fort Orange</a>, later New York City, Kingston and Albany. In 1664 the British took New Netherland, which became New York.</p><p>Under British rule the valley became an agricultural hub and the breadbasket of colonial America, its grain shipped down to New York City. On the east bank, landlords let land to tenants who were often kept at a subsistence level, while smaller farms lay west of the river. In the Revolutionary War the British plan of General John Burgoyne aimed to control the water route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> to New York City and cut off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, and many battles were fought along the river.</p><p>In 1807 the North River Steamboat, later known as Clermont, became the first commercially successful steamboat, carrying passengers between New York City and Albany. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a> opened the valley to the trade of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwest</a>, hundreds of factories rose in towns such as Poughkeepsie, Newburgh and Kingston, and by the end of the 19th century 130 brickyards employing 8,000 people made the region the world's largest producer of bricks. Many industrial towns declined in the mid-20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The valley is divided into the Lower, Mid- and Upper Hudson, and the Hudson River Valley National Heritage Area promotes its historic, natural and cultural sites in 11 counties. It is one of the oldest wine-growing regions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with its first vineyards planted in 1677 at present-day New Paltz, and orchards are common in Orange, Ulster, Dutchess and Columbia counties.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hudson Valley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A large landholding elite owned <b>Hudson Valley</b> manors, and New York City grew as a trading port with many enslaved Africans.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-new-york">Province of New York</a></li></ul></section>
